If $r$ is the radius of the base of the cone, then
\[r^2+d^2=1^2,\]and the height of the cone is
\[h=1+d.\]Therefore, the volume of the cone is
\[V=\frac\pi3r^2h=\frac\pi3(1-d^2)(1+d)=\frac\pi3(1-d)(1+d)^2.\]Thus, we want to maximize $(1-d)(1+d)^2$.

We need a constraint between the three factors of this expression, and this expression is a product.  Let's try to apply the AM-GM inequality by noting that
\[(1-d)+\frac{1+d}2+\frac{1+d}2=2.\]Then
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac23\right)^3 &= \left[\frac{(1-d)+\frac{1+d}2+\frac{1+d}2}3\right]^3 \\
&\geq(1-d)\cdot\frac{1+d}2\cdot\frac{1+d}2,
\end{align*}so
\[
(1-d)(1+d)(1+d)\leq4\left(\frac23\right)^3=\frac{32}{27}.
\]and
\[V=\frac\pi3(1-d)(1+d)^2\leq \frac{\pi}3\cdot\frac{32}{27}= \frac{32\pi}{81}.\]The volume is maximized when the AM-GM inequality is an equality.  This occurs when
\[1-d=\frac{1+d}2=\frac{1+d}2\]so $d=\frac13.$  In this case $h=\frac43$ and
\[r=\sqrt{1-d^2}=\sqrt{\frac89}.\]Indeed, in this case
\[V=\frac\pi3r^2h=\frac\pi3\cdot\frac89\cdot\frac43=\boxed{\frac{32\pi}{81}}.\]

Let $P$ and $Q$ be the the intersection points of the two parabolas.  Then by definition of the parabola, the distance from $P$ to their common focus $F$ is equal to the distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis.  Also, the distance between $P$ to $F$ is equal to $P$ to the $y$-axis.  This means $P$ is equidistant to both the $x$-axis and $y$-axis, so $P$ must lie on the line $y = -x.$

[asy]
unitsize(0.15 cm);

pair F, P, Q;

real parab (real x) {
  return(-(x^2 - 6*x + 793)/56);
}

real upperparab(real x) {
  return(sqrt(3)*sqrt(2*x - 3) - 28);
}

real lowerparab(real x) {
  return(-sqrt(3)*sqrt(2*x - 3) - 28);
}

F = (3,-28);
P = (18.0385,-18.0385);
Q = (43.9615,-43.9615);

draw((-10,0)--(50,0));
draw((0,-50)--(0,10));
draw(graph(parab,-10,47),red);
draw(graph(upperparab,3/2,50),blue);
draw(graph(lowerparab,3/2,50),blue);
draw(F--P--(P.x,0));
draw(P--(0,P.y));
draw(F--Q--(Q.x,0));
draw(Q--(0,Q.y));
draw((-10,10)--(50,-50),dashed);

label("$F$", F, NW, UnFill);
label("$P$", P, NE, UnFill);
label("$Q$", Q, NE, UnFill);

dot(F);
[/asy]

By the same argument, $Q$ also lies on the line $y = -x.$  Therefore, the slope of $\overline{PQ}$ is $\boxed{-1}.$

problem:
Find $a+b+c$, given that $x+y\neq -1$ and  \begin{align*}
ax+by+c&=x+7,\\
a+bx+cy&=2x+6y,\\
ay+b+cx&=4x+y.
\end{align*}

reference_solution:
Since these equations look symmetric, at least on the left-hand side, we add all three together.  This gives us  \begin{align*}
&a(x+1+y)+b(y+x+1)+c(1+y+x)\\
&\qquad\qquad=x+7+2x+6y+4x+y.
\end{align*} We then factor out $(1+x+y)$ on the left and simplify the right side to get \begin{align*}
(1+x+y)(a+b+c)&=7x+7y+7, \text{ so}\\
(1+x+y)(a+b+c)&=7(1+x+y).
\end{align*}

Since $x+y\neq -1$, we know $x+y+1\neq 0$, so we can divide both sides by that term and find that $a+b+c=\boxed{7}$.

